    "problem_statement": "# `one()` function returns incorrect results\n\n## Description\n\nThe `one()` function in funcy/colls.py is not working as expected. It should check whether exactly one item in a sequence passes a predicate (or is truthy), but it's currently returning the opposite result.\n\n## Example\n\n```python\nfrom funcy import one\n\n# These should return True but return False\none([0, False, 3, ''])  # Only one truthy value (3)\none(_ > 0, [0, 1])      # Only one value greater than 0\n\n# These should return False but return True\none([0, False, ''])     # No truthy values\none([1, False, 'a'])    # Multiple truthy values\none(_ < 0, [0, 1, 2])   # No values less than 0\none(_ > 0, [0, 1, 2])   # Multiple values greater than 0\n```\n\n## How to reproduce\n\nHere's a simple script to reproduce the issue:\n\n```python\nfrom funcy import one\n\n# Should be True (only one truthy value)\nresult1 = one([0, False, 3, ''])\nprint(f\"one([0, False, 3, '']) = {result1}\")  # Prints False, should be True\n\n# Should be False (no truthy values)\nresult2 = one([0, False, ''])\nprint(f\"one([0, False, '']) = {result2}\")  # Prints True, should be False\n\n# Should be False (multiple truthy values)\nresult3 = one([1, False, 'a'])\nprint(f\"one([1, False, 'a']) = {result3}\")  # Prints True, should be False\n\n# With predicate\nresult4 = one(lambda x: x > 0, [0, 1])\nprint(f\"one(lambda x: x > 0, [0, 1]) = {result4}\")  # Prints False, should be True\n```\n\nThe issue appears to be in the implementation of the `one()` function, which is incorrectly checking the number of items that pass the predicate.\n\n<END WRITING>"
